---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeoToc from '../../components/seo/SeoToc.astro';
import SeoFaq from '../../components/seo/SeoFaq.astro';
import LateFeeCalculator from '../../components/tools/LateFeeCalculator';

const pageSlug = '/tools/invoice-late-fee-calculator';
const canonicalUrl = `https://chaseai.app${pageSlug}`;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Tools', href: '/tools' },
  { label: 'Invoice Late Fee Calculator', href: pageSlug },
];
const metaTitle = 'Free Invoice Late Fee Calculator — How Much to Charge | ChaseAI';
const metaDescription =
  'Calculate how much late fee or interest you can charge on an overdue invoice. Supports fixed fees and monthly interest. Includes suggested invoice wording. Free, no sign-up.';
const tocItems = [
  { id: 'calculator', label: 'Calculator' },
  { id: 'how-to-calc', label: 'How to calculate' },
  { id: 'standard-rates', label: 'Standard rates by region' },
  { id: 'by-state', label: 'Late fees by US state' },
  { id: 'relationships', label: 'Client relationships' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQ' },
];
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Can I charge late fees if this was not in my contract?', a: 'In the UK, statutory B2B interest can apply automatically. In many US states, contractual terms are generally required.' },
  { q: 'Is 1.5% per month too high?', a: '1.5% monthly equals 18% annualized and is within common commercial ranges in many markets.' },
  { q: 'What is the UK debt recovery charge?', a: 'It is fixed compensation on top of interest: £40, £70, or £100 depending on invoice size.' },
  { q: 'What is the maximum invoice late fee by state?', a: 'Most US states do not set a single cap for late fees on business-to-business invoices. Enforceability usually depends on the fee being agreed in your terms and being reasonable. Consumer transactions and government contracts follow different rules, so confirm your state\u2019s current law before charging.' },
];
const structuredData = [
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'SoftwareApplication', name: 'Invoice Late Fee Calculator', applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication', operatingSystem: 'Web', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' } },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })) },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={metaTitle} description={metaDescription} canonical={canonicalUrl} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} structuredData={structuredData}>
  <section class="hero-gradient">
    <div class="container py-14 max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold">Invoice Late Fee Calculator — Calculate What You’re Owed for Late Payment</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-muted-foreground">This <strong>late payment fee calculator</strong> works out fixed, percentage, or statutory calculations and gives copy-ready invoice wording.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[210px_1fr_180px] gap-10 max-w-6xl mx-auto">
        <aside><SeoToc items={tocItems} /></aside>
        <article class="prose prose-neutral max-w-none prose-a:text-primary">
          <h2 id="calculator">Invoice late fee calculator</h2>
          <div class="not-prose my-5"><LateFeeCalculator client:visible /></div>
          <div class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-primary/20 bg-primary/5 p-4 text-sm">
            <p>Add fee terms once, then automate reminders so clients act before extra charges stack up.</p>
            <a href="https://app.chaseai.app" class="mt-2 inline-flex rounded-lg bg-primary px-4 py-2 font-semibold text-primary-foreground">Start free trial</a>
          </div>

          <h2 id="how-to-calc">How to calculate a late fee on an invoice</h2>
          <p>Fixed fee is simple and predictable. Monthly percentage scales better for larger invoices. UK statutory interest can be legally enforceable for B2B invoices under the Late Payment Act.</p>

          <h2 id="standard-rates">What late fee rate is standard</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>US: no federal statutory rate; 1–2% monthly is common (state law applies).</li>
            <li>UK: Bank of England base rate + 8% statutory for qualifying B2B debts.</li>
            <li>EU: Late Payment Directive sets 8% above ECB reference for B2B.</li>
            <li>Australia: penalty interest often defined by state-level frameworks.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 id="by-state">Late fees by US state: what actually applies</h2>
          <p>
            Searches like "Illinois late fee calculator" or "New York late fee calculator" usually assume each
            state publishes one maximum rate. For business-to-business invoices, that is rarely how it works.
            Three rules cover most situations:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Your contract controls.</strong> A late fee you both agreed to in writing, at a reasonable rate, is generally enforceable in most states.</li>
            <li><strong>Usury caps mostly target loans</strong>, not late fees on invoices for delivered work — but an extreme fee can still be challenged as a penalty.</li>
            <li><strong>Different rules for consumers and government work.</strong> Consumer-protection laws may cap fees charged to individuals, and state prompt-payment acts set interest for public contracts.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="not-prose my-5 overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <caption class="sr-only">Common questions about invoice late fees in frequently searched US states</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
                  <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">State</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Single statutory cap for B2B invoice late fees?</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-semibold">What to check</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="border-b border-border/60">
                  <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">New York</th>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4">No single cap for agreed B2B fees</td>
                  <td class="py-2">Reasonableness of the rate; consumer rules if billing individuals</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-border/60">
                  <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Illinois</th>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4">No single cap for agreed B2B fees</td>
                  <td class="py-2">Contract wording; Illinois prompt-payment rules for public work</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="border-b border-border/60">
                  <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Pennsylvania</th>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4">No single cap for agreed B2B fees</td>
                  <td class="py-2">Contract wording; separate rules for consumer and public contracts</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Wisconsin</th>
                  <td class="py-2 pr-4">No single cap for agreed B2B fees</td>
                  <td class="py-2">Consumer Act limits if billing individuals; contract wording for B2B</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>Confirm before you charge:</strong> state law changes and the details above are general
            guidance, not legal advice. Check your state's current statutes or ask a local attorney, then put the
            agreed fee in your payment terms using proper
            <a href="/templates/late-payment-fee-email-template">invoice late fee wording</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 id="relationships">Does charging a late fee damage client relationships?</h2>
          <p>It can if it is a surprise. If agreed up front in clear payment terms, it is usually treated as professional policy rather than personal conflict.</p>
          <p>Set terms first with the <a href="/tools/payment-terms-generator">Payment Terms Generator</a>, then estimate exposure in the <a href="/tools/late-payment-cost-calculator">Late Payment Cost Calculator</a>.</p>

          <h3>External references</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1998/20/contents" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UK Late Payment of Commercial Debts Act 1998</a></li>
            <li><a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32011L0007" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU Late Payment Directive 2011/7/EU</a></li>
            <li><a href="https://www.sba.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">US SBA: Getting paid on time resources</a></li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Written by Pasko Djonovic, Founder at ChaseAI • Last updated May 6, 2026</strong></p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="faq" class="section-padding border-t border-border bg-muted/30">
    <div class="container max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-center mb-8">FAQ</h2>
      <SeoFaq items={faqs} />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section-padding border-t border-border">
    <div class="container max-w-2xl mx-auto text-center">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold">Apply fair fees with confidence</h2>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col sm:flex-row justify-center gap-3">
        <a href="#calculator" class="inline-flex rounded-lg bg-primary px-7 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-primary-foreground">Calculate late fee</a>
        <a href="https://app.chaseai.app" class="inline-flex rounded-lg border border-border px-7 py-3 text-sm font-semibold">Automate follow-up</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
